job-object failure at the kernel32 boundary only. They were first run on the frozen 1.7.2 code on Windows: the 5 `test_w_...` tests fail by their output (with no job object, or with a process that did not join it, the session ran, its child outlived the supervisor and connected to the stub afterwards; an unreadable count was reported as nothing left running and no problem), and the 2 control tests pass (Windows leaves a child running when its parent exits, and `taskkill /T` on the parent cannot reach it; a working job object ends such a child). On 1.7.4 all 7 pass, and a refused process is shown, just before its end, as it was created: one thread, suspended once, no time charged, and no resume asked for. (Its CPU time after its end is not evidence: Windows may charge one clock tick of user time to a process terminated without ever running.) On Linux and macOS the tree is still a process group; the 3 tests that are not Windows-only run there too. The first build of 1.7.4, never published, failed `mypy` on Linux and macOS with three `name-defined` errors: its Windows-only start used names defined only on Windows, and a type check run on Windows reads the code for Windows alone. This build puts that code under its platform check; what runs on each platform is unchanged. Nothing else changed: the steps, the response checks, the payloads, the limits, the T05 replay checks of 1.7.3 and the live refusal are as before.
 
 Version 1.7.3 corrects the offline replay's handling of its input (T05). Up to 1.7.2, `replay` created its output folder and an offline ledger before it read the saved responses, and an input it could not use ended in a traceback: a missing `replay.json` or a missing `body_file` in a `FileNotFoundError` (exit 1), `{}` in a `KeyError` (exit 1); a file that was not JSON was refused (exit 2) but only after the folder and the ledger had been created. Now every input is checked first: a missing fixtures folder or `replay.json`, a file that cannot be read, is not UTF-8 or is not JSON, a structure other than `{"steps": {step: [response, ...]}}`, an `http_status` that is not an integer from 100 to 599, a `body` that is not text, a response with both `body` and `body_file`, and a `body_file` that is missing, unreadable or outside the fixtures folder are each refused with one line naming the problem (`{"refused": "ReplayRefused: ..."}`, exit 2), and nothing is created; missing `--targets` or `--rates` files likewise. A valid replay behaves as before. The 23 tests of `tests/test_t05_replay_input.py` were first run on the frozen 1.7.2 code: 22 fail by their output (tracebacks, exit 1, an output folder created) and the control test of a valid replay passes; on 1.7.3 all 23 pass. Nothing else changed: the steps, the response checks, the payloads, the limits and the live refusal are those of 1.7.2.
 
